@CookieBadger I was talking about performance because when it comes to mobile rendering and compatibility, it's a very sensitive subject. That's why many advanced features found in Forward+ rendering are left out of these renders.

You'll probably need to make a highly optimized version for these renders and prove with extensive testing that it's feasible on mobiles that support Vulkan or OpenGL, as well as on very old PC hardware that only runs Godot in compatibility mode because it lacks Vulkan or DirectX 12.

I'm not an expert, and I won't have the final say, so it would be better if @clayjohn gave you his opinion since he knows more about this.

@LiveTrower 
Your concerns regarding new LUTs for anisotropy or spherical area lights are correct. However, I do need to point out, that these effects don't work "out of the box" for the most-representative-point method (used in WickedEngine) either. In fact, it does not even provide an accurate fresnel effect, and so metallic materials don't even reflect properly. Plus, it also adds a bunch of additional code and a whole lot of error-prone geometric calculations, and as far as I found out, it yields artifacts at shallow angles (as those seen in my thesis).

Regarding the different light types, I would argue that elliptical lights can be very common and useful, e.g., for things like headlights, and also diverse artistic effects.

While testing a screen-space contact shadows shader, I noticed `light()` doesn't run in custom shaders when using an AreaLight3D node, unlike other light types. How difficult would it be to implement this? (I'd say this can be left for a future PR if it's nontrivial.)

You can test this with this function, which should tint the whole scene red whenever there's any light in view:

```glsl
void light() {
	DIFFUSE_LIGHT += vec3(1.0, 0.0, 0.0);
}
```

